WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:11.160
The vivid imaginations of H.G. Wells and Buck Rogers never cooked up a more fantastic experience

00:11.160 --> 00:16.180
than the Army engineers at their laboratory in Belmar, New Jersey. Banks of instrument

00:16.180 --> 00:23.140
panels control a radar installation that gives man his first actual contact with the moon.

00:23.140 --> 00:33.680
An amazing round trip of over 477,000 miles. Ready to shoot the moon, the radar antenna

00:33.680 --> 00:50.540
is pointed directly at Luna and the thrill of a lifetime is in the making. Three two-and-a-half

00:50.540 --> 01:02.200
second trips up and back. If you're ready for another trip to the moon, let's go. That

01:02.200 --> 01:08.720
was a 180-cycle note, just about like your home radio. It opens up unlimited possibilities

01:08.720 --> 01:15.000
for interstellar experiments, according to Colonel DeWitt, who supervised the project.

01:15.000 --> 01:20.520
Calculations showed that radar equipment could be put together which would reach the

01:20.520 --> 01:32.520
moon and retain. If one allows the imagination free rein, many future possibilities appear.

01:32.520 --> 01:39.960
